00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
this is a production of cornell  university  hello my name is carl scamenti with the  cornell turfgrass program  back with another video in our series  highlighting our erp project  which aims to improve best management  practice adoption at golf courses in new  york state and this video series has  been focusing on  bmps being implemented at golf courses  in our region  today we're going to focus on battle  island golf course which is a state park  18-hole facility  uh  up in fulton new york about 45 minutes  north of syracuse  and so what they're doing up at battle  island  is some pretty basic measuring of  putting surface growth rate uh and i  think the place to start here is first  of all  to explain why it's important to measure  growth rate  i think one thing most superintendents  do  is to make decisions on fertilizer you  know rates and timings based on the  amount of growth they're seeing right so  instead of just a scheduled you know  tenth of a pound of n every two weeks or  every week  being able to be flexible  reacting to growth if growth is too high  scaling back on fertilizer and  you know conversely if growth is too low  scaling up i think most superintendents  have a  knowledge of that and they have a  general sense of growth rates um whether  that's  asking your operator in the morning how  many times you dumped  the baskets right or  or if you're just looking at in the  baskets to see the  amount of clipping buildup  that's a general sense of growth but i  think what we've wondered is is that a  good enough sense  and after doing some growth rate stuff  in the last couple years i think the  analogy i would draw is  um it's kind of like this general sense  is kind of like asking a tour pro in a  tour event  um when they're approaching when they're  hitting their approach out into the  green  that instead of getting exact yardage  hey just round to the nearest yardage  marker  um and of course for a tour pro rounding  to  you know 150 or 200 yards  isn't going to work out for them right  they're very precise they're very  accurate and how they can control their  distance it's why they're talking about  you know 177 or 178  they have the tools to be very exact  they have the skill to be very exact  and because of that they need an exact  yardage and i think when we talk about a  general sense of growth  i think we have the tools to be very  precise in how we control growth and  that necessitates a very accurate  measurement of growth  and one way you can do that is by  measuring clipping volume  this is very simple as you can see we  have a bucket  and what you do for putting greens is  you mow the green you dump the grass in  the bucket  you measure you know how what volume is  in there  um you record it and you track it  right very simple and  and this is what we set up at battle  island uh here's the 16th green that  we did our growth measurements on in  2021  you know a couple reasons we chose the  screen one it's about an average  performing green on the golf course so  it's fairly representative  uh and two you can see in the background  here there's a there's a bath house that  has a  uh storage space and that's where we  kept the the clipping bucket and the  recording sheet so that it stays dry and  that  you know when the mower finishes mowing  the screen we can drive right over there  dump the grass in the bucket get a good  measurement  and be able to repeat that process on a  daily basis  so we did this for for uh 2021 growing  season and  this is the data  so you can see the data is transformed  into a milliliters per meter squared  number so that's basically the volume of  the grass  divided by the the area  of the of the green if you were to  measure two or three greens this is a  way that you can  equalize the growth rates and compare  them apples to apples right because some  greens are bigger and smaller  and you can see kind of one of the  the most easiest things to observe here  in this data is  a lot of variability right and this is  the case with  almost every  uh course we've seen clipping data for  um there's high points there's low  points  so what do we what do you really do with  the data first of all um  you know i think the place to start is  just being  kind of aware during the season of when  the ideal putting surface  conditions when those are occurring at  what growth rates  um so a battle islands between 5 and 15  milliliters per meter squared  at most golf courses you'll be able to  pretty early on  identify when your greens are performing  the best  and at what growth rates  you can achieve those at  what happens is if you're below these  growth rates right right here  that typically happens during tournament  conditions right you're stressing the  turf it's going to grow less  you're going to get great playing  conditions then but can you sustain that  right can you sustain the traffic and  the wear and tear on the turf when it's  not growing that much right probably not  on the other end of things if you're if  you're above that sort of goldilocks  zone  you'll get slower putting greens right  it's growing more you'll get more  organic matter accumulation right that  means softer greens conditions  um  so you know i think the first thing to  notice if you're going to do this is  just observe you know at what growth  rates you're achieving the ideal playing  surface conditions  and then it's time to look at okay why  are we getting  growth rates outside of those  right and you can look at your your  plant growth regulator program your  fertilizer applications  but one thing i've found with  any course that has native push-up soil  greens at least up here in new york  is there's a very strong correlation  between large rain events  and spikes in growth rate  right and the reason for that is most  likely the mineralization of  uh that native soil that native organic  matter material  right as you get big rain events there's  a lot of moisture especially in here in  the summer where there's  enough warmth in the system too  you get that microbial community just  mineralizing a ton of nitrogen it's  pumping nitrogen into the turf  and you get these spikes and growth  right after large rain events right  so this is something that that i would  encourage most people to just kind of  look at  their first year of clipping volume  collection  and just observe the first year right  and that's what we did at battle island  just kind of observed figure out hey  where can we make adjustments to improve  our playing conditions  and then you kind of come up with a plan  and start to implement a couple things  in the next year so  a couple of things you could do  right you could increase your pgr use  whether that's upping the rates to  achieve more  growth suppression and maybe using a  different plant growth regulator that  gives you more  suppression  obviously you're going to look at your  fertilizer rates and timings right if  you're seeing a growth spike in your  data hey hold off on that next nitrogen  application  um moisture management is critical right  so  as we saw there there's a pretty good  correlation between  uh large rain events and spikes in  growth so any way you can kind of  improve your moisture management you  know wetting agents that probably means  cultural practices right getting some of  that native soil out of the profile  i'm getting more sand in there so you  can control moisture  a little bit better  these are all things that  you can kind of implement as you go  along as you see the data  and hopefully will lead to  a more accurate  control of your growth rate and that  means more control over your putting  surface conditions  so uh you know for more information  about this case study we have a link  below to the document that will explain  a little bit more  and go through the data a bit more i  hope you learned something today and  please check out our other videos in our  erp series  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

